<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Praja Mandal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1920s–1940s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Praja Mandals were popular movements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> from the 1920s to the 1940s, linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> through the All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> States Peoples' Conference. They campaigned against forced labour, heavy taxes and arbitrary rule, and for responsible government. Their agitations, at times met with firing and arrests, intensified in 1938–1939 and again after 1946, pressing rulers to merge their states into India after independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…ministers drawn from the popular movements: Gokul Lal Asawa headed the first government at Kota, Manikya Lal Varma the government formed at Udaipur, and Hiralal Shastri of the Jaipur <b>Praja Mandal</b> became the first premier of Greater Rajasthan in 1949, though the transitional governments were not yet elected.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1939 a march by the Dhami Prem Pracharini Sabha and <b>Praja Mandal</b> activists demanding reforms was fired on near Halog, killing protesters, an event remembered in the movement for democratic rule in the hill states.</blockquote><a href="/state/dhami-state">Dhami State</a></li><li><blockquote>Following widespread popular agitation (the <b>Praja Mandal</b> movement) in the Orissa states, its ruler signed the merger agreement and Hindol joined the province of Orissa on 1 January 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/hindol-state">Hindol State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1946–1947 the <b>Praja Mandal</b> led protests against the Raja, and Adivasi unrest followed.</blockquote><a href="/state/nilgiri-state">Nilgiri State</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1939, during agitation by the <b>Praja Mandal</b> against the Raja's exactions, a crowd killed the Political Agent, Major R. L. Bazalgette; the rising was put down and leaders were hanged.</blockquote><a href="/state/ranpur-state">Ranpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1948 the Suket Satyagraha, led by local <b>Praja Mandal</b> activists, forced Raja Lakshman Sen to cede control.</blockquote><a href="/state/suket-state">Suket State</a></li></ul></section>
